{
  "id": "p3",
  "title": "Input and Formatting",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "p3-l1",
      "title": "readln",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "readln(x) waits until the user types something and presses Enter, and stores it in x. Put a write before it, so the user knows what to type. What the user types appears right after the question, and Enter starts a new line.",
        "One readln can read several numbers from the same line: readln(a, b) reads two numbers separated by a space.",
        "If the user types letters where a number is expected, the program stops with Runtime error 106: Invalid numeric format."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Greet;",
          "var",
          "  name: string;",
          "  age: integer;",
          "begin",
          "  write('What is your name? ');",
          "  readln(name);",
          "  write('How old are you? ');",
          "  readln(age);",
          "  writeln('Hello, ', name, '! Next year you will be ', age + 1, '.');",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "What is your name? Niklaus",
          "How old are you? 13",
          "Hello, Niklaus! Next year you will be 14."
        ],
        "inputs": [
          "Niklaus",
          "13"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p3-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does readln(x) do?",
          "options": [
            "Waits for the user to type a value and stores it in x",
            "Prints x",
            "Reads a file",
            "Deletes x"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "It reads one line of input."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why is there a write before readln?",
          "options": [
            "So the user knows what to type",
            "readln needs it to work",
            "To make a new line",
            "It doesn't belong there"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Without a prompt, the screen would just wait silently."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Read two numbers from one line.",
          "code": [
            "  readln(a___ b);"
          ],
          "options": [
            ",",
            ";",
            " +",
            ":"
          ],
          "answer": ",",
          "explanation": "The variables are separated by commas."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What happens if the user types abc for an integer?",
          "options": [
            "Runtime error 106: Invalid numeric format",
            "x becomes 0",
            "x becomes abc",
            "Nothing"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The program stops."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l1-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "The app types 4 and 5 on one line. What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Sum;",
            "var",
            "  a, b: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  readln(a, b);",
            "  writeln(a + b);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "9",
            "45",
            "4 5",
            "20"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "readln(a, b) reads both numbers, and a + b is 9."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l1-e6",
          "type": "order_lines",
          "prompt": "Put the lines in order: ask for a number and print its double.",
          "lines": [
            "write('Number? ');",
            "readln(n);",
            "writeln(n * 2);"
          ],
          "answer": [
            "write('Number? ');",
            "readln(n);",
            "writeln(n * 2);"
          ],
          "explanation": "Ask, read, then calculate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3-l2",
      "title": "Width and Decimals",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "write and writeln can format values: x:8 prints x right-aligned in 8 columns. For reals, x:8:2 also rounds to 2 decimals. x:0:2 uses just as much space as needed.",
        "This also works for strings and integers: name:10 pads the name to 10 characters on the left, which lines up tables.",
        "Without a format, a real comes out as 1.2345678900E+01. So almost every Pascal program writes reals with :0:2 or similar."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Receipt;",
          "var",
          "  price: real;",
          "begin",
          "  price := 4.5;",
          "  writeln('Disks', 3:6, (3 * price):10:2);",
          "  writeln('Manual', 1:5, 29.95:10:2);",
          "  writeln(price);",
          "  writeln(price:0:2);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "Disks     3     13.50",
          "Manual    1     29.95",
          " 4.5000000000E+00",
          "4.50"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p3-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does writeln(3.14159:0:2) print?",
          "options": [
            "3.14",
            "3.14159",
            "3.1",
            " 3.1415900000E+00"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Two decimals, rounded, with no extra space."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does writeln(42:5) print?",
          "options": [
            "   42",
            "42   ",
            "42.00",
            "00042"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Right-aligned in 5 columns: three spaces, then 42."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l2-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Print the price with 2 decimals.",
          "code": [
            "  writeln(price___);"
          ],
          "options": [
            ":0:2",
            ":2",
            "%.2f",
            ".2"
          ],
          "answer": ":0:2",
          "explanation": ":0:2 means: no fixed width, 2 decimals."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "program Third;",
            "begin",
            "  writeln(8 / 3:0:2);",
            "end."
          ],
          "options": [
            "2.67",
            "2.66",
            "2.6666666667E+00",
            "3"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "8 / 3 is 2.666…, rounded to 2 decimals: 2.67."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l2-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Fmt;",
            "var",
            "  n: integer;",
            "begin",
            "  n := 7;",
            "  writeln(n:0:2);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 5,
          "explanation": "Only reals can have decimals. An integer gets just a width, like n:4."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why do Pascal programs write reals with :0:2 so often?",
          "options": [
            "Without a format they come out in scientific notation",
            "It makes them faster",
            "It's required",
            "To make them integers"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "1.2345678900E+01 is hard to read."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p3-l3",
      "title": "Constants",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Values that never change get a name in the const section, before var: const VAT = 0.19; Note the = (not :=): a constant is defined, not assigned.",
        "Trying to change a constant later is an error. That protects values like conversion factors.",
        "Pascal already knows pi and maxint (32767)."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "program Circle;",
          "const",
          "  Radius = 2.5;",
          "var",
          "  area: real;",
          "begin",
          "  area := pi * Radius * Radius;",
          "  writeln('Area: ', area:0:3);",
          "  writeln('Biggest integer: ', maxint);",
          "end."
        ],
        "output": [
          "Area: 19.635",
          "Biggest integer: 32767"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "p3-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How is a constant defined?",
          "options": [
            "const VAT = 0.19;",
            "const VAT := 0.19;",
            "var VAT = 0.19;",
            "VAT := 0.19;"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Constants use = in the const section."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where does the const section go?",
          "options": [
            "Before begin, usually before var",
            "After end.",
            "Between begin and end",
            "Anywhere"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "All declarations come before the main begin."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l3-e3",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This program doesn't compile. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "program Fixed;",
            "const",
            "  Max = 10;",
            "begin",
            "  Max := 20;",
            "  writeln(Max);",
            "end."
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "A constant can't be changed."
        },
        {
          "id": "p3-l3-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Define the constant.",
